          "llm": {
            "status": "clean",
            "verdict": "benign",
            "normalizedStatus": "clean",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill's files and runtime instructions are consistent with a multi‑agent orchestration installer — it will modify OpenClaw agent config, create agent workspaces, and optionally install a background watcher; review scripts and consent before running.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The name/description (multi‑agent DAG dispatch, durable tracking, retries) matches what the files and SKILL.md actually do: copy agent templates, create per‑agent workspaces, write agent entries into openclaw.json, and set up task tracking. There are no unrelated credentials or external services required by default.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "ok"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md requires the agent/operator to run the included setup scripts and to 'must first execute' an environment detection/installation step. The instructions explicitly tell the agent to derive SKILL_DIR, create symlinks, copy assets to ~/.openclaw/agents/<id>, write ~/.openclaw/configs/team-dispatch.json and modify/openclaw.json, and restart the Gateway. Those are high‑impact operations (write/modify system/user agent configuration and restart a service) — they are coherent with the skill's stated purpose but are intrusive and should be consented to and audited first. The skill also documents installing a low‑frequency watcher (background job) which implies persistent background behavior.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "There is no network install spec; everything is packaged as local scripts and assets. That reduces remote code fetch risk, but the included scripts will perform filesystem writes, create symlinks, and install/enable a watcher via system schedulers (openclaw-cron/launchd/systemd/cron). Installing scheduled/background jobs and rendering system scheduler units is a nontrivial installer action — review the scripts (watch-install, setup.sh, restart steps) before executing.",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill declares no required environment variables or credentials. The docs reference provider model names (openai-codex/gpt-5.2, etc.) and troubleshooting mentions OPENAI_API_KEY if the user enables an OpenAI provider, but that is optional and user‑supplied later. Notification integrations (Telegram) appear optional and disabled by default. Overall requested environment access is proportionate to the stated functionality.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "ok"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The scripts will create persistent state under the user's home (~/.openclaw/skills, ~/.openclaw/agents, ~/.openclaw/configs, backups, tasks) and may install a background watcher (system scheduler). They will also modify openclaw.json and restart the Gateway to apply changes. While this persistence is needed for a durable dispatcher, it is intrusive and grants the skill long‑lived presence and the ability to affect agent behavior and background processing — inspect and back up existing openclaw.json/configs before running setup.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
